<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thrasybulus of Miletus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thrasybulus was the tyrant of Miletus in the late 7th and early 6th centuries BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> relates that during a long Lydian war he deceived King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a> by heaping up grain and holding feasts while the Lydian envoy was in the city, convincing him that Miletus could not be starved out and so winning peace. He was a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander of Corinth</a>, to whom he gave advice on ruling by cutting down the tallest ears of corn.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 390 BCE the Athenian general <b>Thrasybulus</b> restored democratic government and imposed a tithe on ships leaving the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Sadyattes and then Alyattes the Lydians invaded Milesian territory every year for eleven years, burning the crops, but could not take the city because Miletus controlled the sea; the tyrant <b>Thrasybulus</b> finally persuaded Alyattes to make peace by staging a show of plenty when a Lydian envoy arrived.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus told how <b>Thrasybulus of Miletus</b>, asked by Periander for advice, said nothing but cut off the tallest ears in a field of corn.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
